# Database — leave empty to use local PGlite (.data/pglite)
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@host/db?sslmode=require
#
# The production database's HOSTNAME (not a URL, and not a credential — safe to set on every
# environment, which is the point). `vercel.json` runs db:migrate in every environment, and
# scripts/migrate.ts writes as well as reconciling DDL, so a preview build that inherited
# production's DATABASE_URL would migrate and backfill live customer data. Setting this arms
# the refusal in checkMigrationTarget (src/lib/env.ts); unset, it warns instead.
# The real fix is scoping DATABASE_URL to Production and giving previews their own Neon branch.
# PRODUCTION_DB_HOST=ep-something-1234.us-east-2.aws.neon.tech
#
# drizzle-kit push/migrate/drop are refused by drizzle.config.ts unless this is 1 AND
# DATABASE_URL is not PRODUCTION_DB_HOST. Set it only for the one command, never in a file.
# ALLOW_DRIZZLE_PUSH=1

# Clerk (optional in development — demo mode uses demo-user; required in production)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# CLERK_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=whsec_... 
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL=/sign-in
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_UP_URL=/sign-up
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_IN_URL=/dashboard
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_AFTER_SIGN_UP_URL=/onboarding

# Stripe — sells the one-time Orbit Lifetime tier AND the recurring Orbit Pro subscription
# (Clerk Billing is no longer used). Every price id must come from the SAME Stripe mode as
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY: a live key with test-mode price ids fails checkout with "No such price".
# Without the Lifetime pair, /pricing shows Lifetime as "not on sale yet" rather than a broken
# button; without both Pro ids, Pro checkout is hidden the same way.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...             # per-endpoint; `stripe listen` prints a different one
# STRIPE_LIFETIME_PRICE_ID=price_...          # PRICE id of the introductory one-off Lifetime product
# STRIPE_LIFETIME_STANDARD_PRICE_ID=price_... # PRICE id charged once the intro seats are sold
#                                             # (src/lib/lifetime-offer.ts); unset = intro price forever
# STRIPE_PRO_MONTHLY_PRICE_ID=price_...       # recurring Pro, monthly
# STRIPE_PRO_ANNUAL_PRICE_ID=price_...        # recurring Pro, annual

# AI — every plan but Lifetime is bring-your-own-key (Settings → Integrations → AI).
# Orbit's MANAGED keys: what Orbit Lifetime accounts run on when they have not added a key
# of their own. Only ever issued by src/lib/ai-access.ts, capped per account per month
# (MANAGED_AI_BUDGET in src/lib/managed-ai-policy.ts). Set at least one in production
# when Lifetime is on sale.
# ORBIT_MANAGED_GEMINI_API_KEY=
# ORBIT_MANAGED_OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ORBIT_MANAGED_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ORBIT_MANAGED_WISPR_API_KEY=
# ORBIT_MANAGED_AI=off            # emergency stop: every Lifetime account falls back to BYOK
#
# Local development only (ignored on Vercel): these act as the managed keys above, so on
# `next dev` — where every account is a demo account and counts as Lifetime — AI runs on
# them. ORBIT_DEMO_MANAGED_AI=off makes localhost behave like production (BYOK unless
# Lifetime), for seeing the "add your key" states.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ORBIT_DEMO_MANAGED_AI=off
#
# `npm test` never sees any of these AI keys, nor APOLLO_API_KEY or RESEND_API_KEY: the smoke
# preamble (scripts/smoke/_env.ts) deletes them so a run can't spend money. Set to 1 in
# the shell (not here) only when a script really needs a live provider.
# SMOKE_ALLOW_PROVIDER_KEYS=1

# Voice-note transcription (optional). Wispr Flow accepts the user's contact names as a
# spoken-word dictionary, which is why voice capture prefers it: a misheard name in a
# networking CRM creates a duplicate contact, not a typo. Without a key, capture falls
# back to Whisper and then Gemini, both of which still get the same names as a prompt.
# NOTE: Wispr's API is reportedly limited to existing partners rather than open signup,
# and the request/response shape in src/lib/wispr.ts could not be verified against the
# live docs — check it there before debugging a failure. Keys: platform.wisprflow.ai
# Users add their own in Settings; this local-dev name acts as a managed key, like the above.
# WISPR_API_KEY=

# Resend delivery webhook (recommended once you are sending real mail).
# Create an endpoint in the Resend dashboard pointing at
#   https://your-domain.com/api/webhooks/resend
# and subscribe it to email.bounced and email.complained. Without this, a dead
# address is mailed forever and a spam complaint changes nothing — which is what
# gets a sending domain throttled, so it protects every email the app sends.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Encrypts user-provided API keys at rest
ENCRYPTION_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# Optional — Microlink Pro key for higher LinkedIn photo lookup quotas
# (free tier is ~25 requests; without a key, Orbit backfills gradually)
# MICROLINK_API_KEY=

# Vercel Blob — recommended home for LinkedIn headshots (avatar backfill).
# Provision via `vercel blob store add` (or the Vercel dashboard) on a linked
# project, then `vercel env pull .env.local` to fill this in. Without it the
# backfill still works, but stores each photo inline on the contact row
# instead of in Blob storage.
# BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=
# On Vercel with a Blob store connected to the project, the store id plus Vercel's
# injected OIDC token also work instead of the read-write token (src/lib/contact-avatar.ts).
# VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN is set by Vercel at runtime and by `vercel env pull` locally.
# BLOB_STORE_ID=store_...
# VERCEL_OIDC_TOKEN=

# The app's public base URL. Effectively required in production: user-facing
# links (the calendar feed you subscribe to in Google/Apple Calendar) are built
# from this, and the Vercel fallback `VERCEL_URL` is the per-deployment host,
# which changes on every deploy. Leave unset locally to default to localhost.
# APP_BASE_URL=https://your-domain.com

# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION — secures the internal job routes (the process-stalled
# cron, import self-continuation, and the embedding / timeline backfill kickers).
# Vercel Cron sends it automatically as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`;
# the app's own kicks and the GitHub Actions scheduler send the same header.
# The routes are fail-closed (src/lib/internal-auth.ts): on Vercel with this
# unset they answer 401 to everyone, including the app itself. Only a local
# `next dev` with no secret leaves them open.
# CRON_SECRET=

# Traffic analytics (optional). The secret behind cookieless visitor identity: a visitor
# is sha256(sha256(ANALYTICS_SALT + today) + ip + user-agent), so no cookie is set and no
# IP is ever stored. Unset, /api/track records nothing and /admin/analytics says so.
#
# Deliberately NOT required: `check:env` gates every production deploy, and an analytics
# secret must never be able to block one. Use 32+ random characters; `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# Changing it resets visitor identity from that day forward — existing rows stay, but
# nothing before and after the change can be compared as the same visitor.
# ANALYTICS_SALT=

# Admin console (optional). Comma- or whitespace-separated Clerk user ids allowed to
# reach /admin. Leave unset and the console does not exist — every /admin route 404s.
#
# Server-only: do NOT prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_, or the allowlist ships to every browser.
# Access additionally requires NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY to be set, so demo mode
# (which resolves every caller to the shared "demo-user") can never grant it.
#
# Find your id in the Clerk dashboard, or on your own account page once signed in.
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=user_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Live-demo cheat code (optional, NOT for production). The Clerk user id that Ctrl+Shift+U
# may grant Orbit Lifetime to, with the plan-celebration animation, instead of a real Stripe
# checkout. Scoped to exactly this one account. src/lib/env.ts refuses a production build
# that sets it, so live demos run on a preview or local deployment. Unset disables it.
# DEMO_ACCOUNT_USER_ID=user_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Localhost demo workspace (optional). On `next dev` every account is a demo account: plan
# gates are lifted, and an account with no contacts is filled with a demo network, timelines,
# reminders, outreach, recruiters, events and chat on its first request. Set to "off" to work
# on onboarding or empty states locally. Has no effect on any deployment.
# ORBIT_DEMO_DATA=off

# Simulated database latency, local PGlite only (optional, measurement). Every statement waits
# this many ms before it runs, the way each neon-http query is its own round trip in
# production — so a page's local render time shows how many queries it awaits in sequence.
# Used with scripts/dev/page-depth.mjs. Has no effect when DATABASE_URL is set.
# ORBIT_SIM_DB_LATENCY_MS=50

# Gmail OAuth (optional — recruiter inbox import + Google Contacts import).
# Use ONE exact redirect URI per environment (must match Google Cloud Console).
# Google Contacts import also requires the People API enabled and
# https://www.googleapis.com/auth/contacts.readonly added to the OAuth consent
# screen's scopes on the same Google Cloud project.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/gmail/callback
# Production:
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://orbit.jasonpereira.live/api/gmail/callback
# Preview/staging: set GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI to that deployment's
#   https://<host>/api/gmail/callback and add the same URI in Google Cloud.

# Microsoft OAuth (optional — Outlook/Microsoft Contacts import).
# Requires an Azure AD (Microsoft Entra ID) app registration: set the redirect
# URI below, add Graph delegated permissions Contacts.Read and User.Read, and
# generate a client secret. Use "common" (default) for both personal and
# work/school accounts, or a specific tenant ID to restrict to one org.
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_ID=
# MICROSOFT_CLIENT_SECRET=
MICROSOFT_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/outlook/callback
# MICROSOFT_TENANT_ID=common

# Eventbrite OAuth (optional — the Eventbrite connection on /events). Create an app at
# https://www.eventbrite.com/platform/api-keys with the redirect below. Without the id and
# secret, "Connect Eventbrite" fails at the OAuth start. The redirect defaults to
# APP_BASE_URL + /api/events/eventbrite/callback when unset.
# EVENTBRITE_CLIENT_ID=
# EVENTBRITE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# EVENTBRITE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/api/events/eventbrite/callback

# Contact form (/contact). All three must be set or the form is hidden and the
# page falls back to the links — a form that always fails is worse than none.
# Sending uses Orbit's own Resend account, never a visitor's key.
#   CONTACT_INBOX_EMAIL — where messages land; the visitor's address is set as
#     Reply-To, so replying in your mail client goes straight back to them.
#   CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL  — the From address, on a domain verified in Resend.
#     Falls back to RESEND_FROM_EMAIL (also used by outreach sending).
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=orbit@your-verified-domain.com
# CONTACT_INBOX_EMAIL=you@your-domain.com
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=contact@your-verified-domain.com

# Hosted outreach (optional). Orbit's own Apollo and Twilio credentials, used only for plans
# with hosted enrichment/sending (src/lib/apollo.ts, src/lib/outreach-send.ts); a user's own
# keys in Settings always win. Unset, those plans silently fall back to "add your own key".
# APOLLO_API_KEY=
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=AC...
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=+15555550123

# Orbit browser extension (optional). Where the "Add to Chrome" button in the
# notifications panel points. Leave unset and it falls back to a Chrome Web
# Store search for Orbit; set it to the listing URL once the extension is
# published there.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_EXTENSION_URL=https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/<slug>/<id>

# Browser extension (optional — the Chrome extension's API at /api/extension/*).
# EXTENSION_ORIGIN is the extension's own origin, added to Clerk's
# authorizedParties so it may present a session to this app. The ID is stable
# because the extension pins it with a `key` field in its manifest.
# Comma-separated, so a dev unpacked build and the published build can both be
# allowed. Note that adding ANY value here switches Clerk from its default
# origin check to an exact list; the host users browse is re-included
# automatically via getAppBaseUrl() (APP_BASE_URL above, or the Vercel
# production domain), so keep APP_BASE_URL accurate in production.
# EXTENSION_ORIGIN=chrome-extension://<extension-id>
#
# Local development only. Without Clerk keys the app runs in demo mode and there
# is no session for the extension to ride, so the extension API accepts this
# shared secret in an `x-orbit-dev-secret` header instead. Ignored unless
# NODE_ENV=development, and never set this in production.
# EXTENSION_DEV_SECRET=
# EXTENSION_DEV_USER_ID=demo-user

# --- Operations: knowing when something breaks -------------------------------------
# Slack incoming webhooks. Everything the ops sweep says goes to the first; CRITICAL
# conditions (Stripe checkout failing, Clerk/Stripe webhooks rejected) ALSO go to the
# second — make that a channel with mobile push set to "all messages". Unset = no alerts.
# SLACK_OPS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
# SLACK_OPS_CRITICAL_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/...
#
# Slack DM to one person (you) — separate from the webhooks above, which can only post
# into a channel. Create a Slack app (api.slack.com/apps) with bot scopes `chat:write`
# and `im:write`, install it to the workspace, and use the Bot User OAuth Token below.
# Get SLACK_ALERT_USER_ID from your Slack profile ("Copy member ID", looks like
# U0123ABC456). Both unset = no DMs; unhandled request errors and new feedback then fall
# back to (or skip, for feedback) the webhook above. Set both, or neither.
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
# SLACK_ALERT_USER_ID=U0123ABC456
#
# Gates the deep view of GET /api/health (?token=...): cron freshness, webhook outcomes,
# open alerts, missing config. The shallow view (up/down + deployed sha) needs no token.
# HEALTH_TOKEN=
#
# Sentry (free tier). Server and client DSNs are the same value; SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN is
# build-time only and enables source-map upload. Unhandled request errors always try the
# Slack DM above (if configured) regardless of Sentry — Sentry keeps the stack trace,
# Slack is the "come look at this now" ping. Unset Sentry AND no Slack DM = falls back to
# the plain webhook above; unset everything = no error tracking at all.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://...@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=https://...@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=orbit
# SENTRY_PROJECT_URL=https://<org>.sentry.io/projects/orbit/
#
# Better Stack heartbeat the sweep pings on success. The monitor alerts when it goes quiet
# for 30 minutes — which is how a dead GitHub schedule shows up.
# BETTERSTACK_HEARTBEAT_URL=https://uptime.betterstack.com/api/v1/heartbeat/...

# Content-Security-Policy. Sent report-only by default (violations land in error_events
# via /api/csp-report). Set to 1 to enforce once a week of reports has stayed quiet.
# CSP_ENFORCE=1

# Provider status panel on /admin/health. Each provider is checked independently and
# degrades to "unconfigured" rather than erroring, so the panel renders with or without
# any of these — Clerk and Stripe already work from the keys above, and Vercel and Neon
# read as unconfigured until the two below are set.
#
# Vercel: a token with read access to the project, from Account Settings -> Tokens.
# VERCEL_API_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_PROJECT_ID=prj_...
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=team_...            # omit on a personal account
#
# Neon: an API key from the Neon console, plus the project the app connects to.
# NEON_API_KEY=
# NEON_PROJECT_ID=
#
# Where each provider's name links to in the panel. Optional — without them the row
# renders as plain text instead of a link to the dashboard you'd actually open next.
# ADMIN_VERCEL_DASHBOARD_URL=https://vercel.com/<team>/<project>
# ADMIN_NEON_DASHBOARD_URL=https://console.neon.tech/app/projects/<id>
# ADMIN_CLERK_DASHBOARD_URL=https://dashboard.clerk.com/apps/<app>
# ADMIN_STRIPE_DASHBOARD_URL=https://dashboard.stripe.com
